# 14. Conclusion

The application was vulnerable to **Web Cache Poisoning** due to the unsafe handling of the `fehost` cookie combined with an improperly configured caching mechanism.

By injecting a specially crafted value into the cookie, it was possible to introduce malicious JavaScript into the cached homepage. Because the cookie was not included in the cache key, the poisoned response was served to other users, resulting in a stored client-side compromise.

This vulnerability highlights the security risks of combining client-controlled input with shared caching mechanisms and demonstrates the importance of both proper output encoding and secure cache configuration.
